These programs are not yet new published paper results. They record the
experiments, decision rules, and exploratory training-data checks needed to
establish or falsify the biological claims; the robust mediation bound and
standard-trajectory equality criteria, positive history-lift theorem, and
prefix-tree endpoint-quotient chain theorem are new mathematical results at
the prospective-research tier. The 2025 nonperturbative source audit adds a
negative result: all eight published 77 K printed rate boxes miss the exact
q79 ideal-cascade rate cone. This removes the simple scalar-rate promotion
from the frontier; it does not exclude a genuinely non-Markovian history or
process-tensor promotion.
The process-witness packet closes the ideal observable-selection row: one
binary cycle-projector test is state-specifically complete and robustly bounds
all downstream outcomes. The coherent-witness-circuit packet gives the exact
three-mode interferometer and its calibration contract. The downstream
history-tester compiler now closes the ideal extraction step as well: a
qutrit tag plus endpoint SWAP reproduces the class-history matrix, 27 physical
CP phase settings recover the full selected state, and 15 recover the witness.
The selected-response theorem further proves that a physical response need not
invert all 15 settings: two calibrated rows spanning witness and capture are
sufficient. It does not claim that an FMO pulse sequence already realizes
those rows.
The structure-derived control packet now removes the next algebraic obstacle:
excitons 1, 2, and 4 are bright, their dipoles span three dimensions in both
deposited monomers, and the ideal rotating-frame dipole controls generate all
of `su(8)`. It simultaneously proves that neither fixed polarization nor a
single transform-limited 50 fs Gaussian pulse realizes the exact projectors.
The physical frontier is therefore bounded, bath-robust shaped control or a
direct calibrated 2DES/heterodyne response, not basic dipole accessibility.
The isotropic 2DES packet removes two further obstructions: ensemble
orientation does not annihilate any selected matrix entry, and frequency
resolution can come from the coherence/detection scan instead of an
impossibly narrow 50-fs pulse. The weakest orientational entry is nonzero but
induces a factor condition number `29.03`; full GSB/SE/ESA pathway mixing and
the physical response covariance remain the next gate.
The downstream optical packets now compute that pathway mixing at the minimal
hard-core null tier and expose why a reduced `chi` shortcut is insufficient for
the retained-memory problem. Direct action on all `38,760` accepted ADOs emits
a positive four-history response whose 15 settings reconstruct capture
`0.0802794382` and cycle witness `0.2947589852` with accepted depth and bath
convergence. The four first-tag filters also have exact `SU(29)` dilations:
their minimum unitary-dilation dimension is 12, the ground-plus-single space is
provably too small, and the full `K_ij=0` optical control fixture generates
`su(29)` with exactly isolated Bohr gaps. The dilations also form exact
two-outcome instruments. The downstream sector theorem now proves that the
standard hard-core site-occupation bath preserves all nine excitation
bigrades at every ADO tier, independently of temperature, Padé count,
hierarchy depth, and number-conserving `K_ij`. Coherent shelving therefore
reproduces the ideal filtered response throughout the complete post-pulse HEOM
wait without a mid-course projection. This closes the abstract filter,
reachability, and post-pulse bath evolution, not its physical pulse
implementation. The remaining control target is specifically a bounded
bath-active pulse error bound for the number-changing dipole interval, with
decay/annihilation omissions, physical `K_ij`, disorder, orientation,
heterodyne covariance, and calibration included.
The finite-pulse response packet now supplies the scalar GSB/SE/ESA layer, and
the full-ADO response packet corrects the control certificate itself. Since
all four ideal phase filters have the same success effect, an adjoint effect
audit cannot establish the phase map even at zero error. For a physical model
`y=R x+N z`, the selected functional is identifiable exactly when `(c,0)` lies
in `row([R N])`. All six stored 50-fs D1 maps reject exact full-ADO
factorization and coefficient-tier nuisance cancellation. Four coherent
endpoint effects have now been executed for every map. Their `24 x 4` actual
response has rank four and cancels one realized outside-source column at
rowspace residuals of order `1e-15`. This is a source-conditioned recovery
result, not full instrument closure: actual-to-ideal error is `0.17602`, the
largest estimator norm is `99.50`. The full `24 x 392` adjoint response is now
also closed. Its 388-column complex nuisance complement has rank 18, equal to
the complete observable rank, and rejects all four coordinate targets with
residuals from `0.99092` to `0.99989`. This closes the ambient-complex nuisance
map negatively; the physical Hermitian HEOM tangent, heterodyne calibration,
and convergence remain open.
The physiological-temperature benchmark packet closes the ambiguity in the
standard comparator input, and its seven-site one-time HEOM baseline is
accepted. The history-functional packet now goes one tier further: on a frozen
bath-clock two-time tester it emits a positive, converged standard history
state with q79 cycle-witness probability `0.2947589852`, versus the ideal q79
value `1`. This is a sharp abstract-observable distinction. Its exact
operational compiler and prospective error margin are now known, but the
physically promoted comparator remains open because the molecular
pulse/heterodyne response map, physiological model error, laboratory clock,
and held-out uncertainty are not yet certified.

